        'synopsis' => 'In 1807 a hot-headed officer curses his country in open court, and the sentence he receives teaches him, across half a century at sea, what that country truly was.',
        'text' => "On the thirteenth of August, 1863, very few readers of the New York Herald noticed an obscure notice among the Deaths. It read, \"NOLAN. Died, on board United States Corvette Levant, on the eleventh of May, Philip Nolan.\" If the officer who sent it had written instead, \"Died, May 11, THE MAN WITHOUT A COUNTRY,\" every eye in America would have stopped there. For fifty years and more that was all the poor fellow had ever been called by the men of the navy who kept him.\n\nNow at last the secret can be told, for it speaks well for the honor of the service that it was kept so long.\n\nIn September of 1807 the whole Mississippi valley seethed with plots and rumors of plots, and Aaron Burr's name stood behind the worst of them. At Fort Adams on the river a court-martial sat trying officers caught up in that treason. Among them came little Nolan, a lieutenant who had grown up hunting wild horses in Texas and who thought little of any government east of the mountains. There was evidence enough against him. He would have marched anywhere under any banner, if only the order were signed \"By command of His Excellency A. Burr.\"\n\nThe president of the court asked him at the close whether he wished to say anything to show that he had always been faithful to the United States. And Nolan, mad with rage, cried out,\n\n\"Damn the United States! I wish I may never hear of the United States again!\"\n\nThe words fell like stones. Old Colonel Morgan, who held the court, had served through the Revolution himself, and half the officers around him had risked their necks for the very idea this boy cursed so lightly. The room went dead still. Morgan retired for fifteen minutes and returned with a face like a sheet.\n\n\"Prisoner,\" said he, \"hear the sentence of the Court. You have wished never to hear the name of the United States again. The Court decides, subject to the approval of the President, that you shall have your wish. Mr. Marshal, see that no one mentions the United States to the prisoner while he lives.\"\n\nNolan laughed at first, but nobody laughed with him, and even he felt his swagger die away. The President approved the sentence. From that day, September 23, 1807, until the day he died, May 11, 1863, Philip Nolan never heard his country's name again.\n\nHe was passed from ship to ship of the navy, given quarters like an officer and guarded like a prisoner, and the orders followed him everywhere. He might read anything but American newspapers. He might go anywhere aboard but ashore in her ports. And no man, on pain of dishonor, should tell him where he was when she lay at home, nor speak her name in his hearing.\n\nSo he lived, year upon year upon year, while the fleet grew old around him. He made himself useful and beloved. He taught mathematics to the young officers and told the boys of the steerage wonderful tales of Texas and the great western rivers. He served his gun bravely the day they took the Java, and won thanks from Captain Porter himself. Every man in the squadron grew gray knowing him, and every man kept the silence.\n\nYet he knew. He knew there was something forbidden, something loved, hanging just out of sight beyond the horizon, and once, long before, he begged an officer to speak of it, saying he had repented a thousand times over. The officer wept as he refused him. It was the hardest duty the navy ever asked of its gentlemen.\n\nAt last, in the spring of 1863, aboard the Levant, Nolan lay down to die. The young captain Danforth went to him and found his cabin turned into a shrine. Above his bed hung the stars and stripes, and beneath them a picture of Washington, and around both an eagle painted by Nolan's own hand, its wings spread over the globe. A great map of the United States, drawn from memory across the decades, lay at his feet.\n\n\"Here, you see,\" said Nolan, smiling, \"I have a country.\"\n\nThen he begged, \"Tell me everything, Danforth, before I die.\" And when the young man faltered he cried, \"There is not in this ship, not in all America, God bless her, a more loyal man than I. There are thirty-four stars in that flag now. I thank God for every one of them, though I do not know their names. There has never been one taken away. By that I know there has never been another Burr.\"\n\nSo Danforth knelt and told him all, the names of the new states, Texas won, California and Oregon reaching to the Pacific, steamboats and railroads and telegraphs, and Grant marching on Vicksburg not a hundred miles above his own Fort Adams. Nolan drank it in like water. He asked who was President now, and whether Old Abe was a Kentuckian like himself, and when he heard how the man had risen from the ranks he cried, \"Good for him!\"\n\nOnly about the war Danforth could not bring his mouth to speak. But Nolan prayed with him anyway, the same collect for the President and all in authority he had repeated morning and night for fifty-five years. Then he kissed the young man and said, \"Look in my Bible, Captain, when I am gone.\"\n\nWithin the hour the doctor found him dead, with a smile on his face and his father's badge of the Order of the Cincinnati pressed to his lips. In the Bible lay a slip of paper marked at the verse, \"They desire a country, even a heavenly.\" Below it he had written, \"Bury me in the sea. It has been my home and I love it. But will not someone set up a stone for my memory at Fort Adams or at Orleans, that my disgrace may be no more than I can bear? Say on it, In memory of Philip Nolan, Lieutenant in the Army of the United States. He loved his country as no other man has loved her, but no man deserved less at her hands.\"",
