        'synopsis' => 'Seven-year-old Benjamin Franklin spends every penny he owns on a tin whistle and learns a lesson about foolish bargains that lasts his whole life.',
        'text' => "Two hundred years ago there lived in Boston a little boy whose name was Benjamin Franklin. On the day he turned seven years old, his mother put a few copper pennies into his hand.\n\nIt was the first money he had ever owned.\n\n\"What shall I do with these coppers?\" he asked.\n\n\"You may buy something if you wish,\" said his mother. \"But be careful not to spend them foolishly.\"\n\nBenjamin ran out into the street, hearing the pennies jingle in his pocket. How rich he felt! He came from a big family, and no one had ever had money for toys. He had never owned one in all his life.\n\nHe had not gone far when he met a bigger boy blowing a whistle. What a pretty sound it made!\n\n\"I have some pennies,\" said Benjamin. \"You may have them all if you will give me that whistle.\"\n\n\"All of them?\" asked the boy.\n\n\"Yes, all of them.\"\n\n\"Well, it's a bargain,\" said the other, and handed over the whistle.\n\nBenjamin ran home blowing it as hard as he could. \"See, Mother!\" he cried. \"I have bought a whistle!\"\n\n\"How much did you pay for it?\"\n\n\"All the pennies you gave me.\"\n\nOne of his brothers took the whistle and laughed. \"Well, well! You paid too much for this thing. It is only a penny whistle, and a poor one at that.\"\n\nHis mother nodded kindly. \"You might have bought half a dozen such whistles with your money.\"\n\nThen Benjamin saw what a mistake he had made. The whistle pleased him no longer. He threw it on the floor and began to cry.\n\n\"Never mind, my child,\" said his mother gently. \"You are only a very little boy, and you will learn a great deal as you grow bigger. Today you have learned never to pay too much for a thing you want.\"\n\nBenjamin Franklin lived to be a very old man, but he never forgot that lesson. All through his long life, whenever he saw people paying too dearly for what they wanted, whether in money or trouble or honor, he would shake his head softly and say to himself, \"Too much for my whistle.\"