        'cover' => 'covers/scifi-the-star.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'On the first day of the new year astronomers find a stranger among the stars, and as it swells toward the sun the whole world learns how small it truly is.',
        'text' => "On the first day of the new year three observatories announced together that Neptune, the outermost planet, was behaving strangely. Its motion had slowed. Ogilvy, who had watched the change since December, went looking for a cause and found something far stranger instead. Close beside Neptune hung a small patch of greenish glow, a wandering scrap of shining gas, and it was rushing toward the sun.\n\nFor some hours the world did not grasp what this meant. Then other observers confirmed the discovery, and the news went everywhere at once. Something vast was coming. It had been dragging at the planets for weeks unseen, and now at last it had been found.\n\nIt grew as it came. The speck became a small round glow, and the glow brightened night after night through January into February. Newspapers printed maps of the sky. Schoolboys learned the word nebula and repeated it knowingly. In every city people climbed to rooftops at dusk to pick out the stranger, one faint point among thousands, terrible only because everyone knew it would not stand still.\n\nThen came the master mathematician. He sat alone with his papers and worked out the visitor's path from the observations, and when he finished he sat still for a long time. His figures showed plainly that on its plunge inward the thing would pass terribly close to this earth. He sent his warning to the newspapers, and the editors called it alarmist and buried it in a few quiet lines. But the warning leaked out anyway, and fear traveled around the world faster than any denial could chase it.\n\nPeople tried to live as before, buying and selling and laughing a little too quickly. Meanwhile the star grew until it burned pale white even at noon. Its heat reached the warmest countries first. Crops withered in the fields. Strange storms gathered over the oceans. In crowded tropical cities men wiped the sweat from their faces and told each other that science must surely be mistaken.\n\nAt midnight the streets glowed like cloudy summer evenings. Churches filled. Rich men fled to mountains that proved no cooler, then to ships, then nowhere at all, for there is nowhere to hide from a sky.\n\nThe mathematician's figures had named the hour of closest approach. At seven-fifteen by Greenwich time the star would hang swollen and blazing above half the world, while the other half slept uneasily in its light.\n\nWhat happened then no man saw whole. The earth groaned and swung a little on its path, as a ship recoils from a blow that misses by a hand's width. Tides rose beyond memory or record and swept deep inland, drowning cities that had stood for centuries and tearing ships from their anchors. Volcanoes answered from every coast. Then came days of darkness and heat, with red dust thickening the air, while earthquakes shook the broken lands for weeks and months together.\n\nBut the star had passed. Slowly, hunger-driven and gathering courage, men crept back to their ruined cities and sodden fields. And as the storms settled they noticed strange mercies written in the sky above them. The days were warmer than of old, and the sun seemed larger, and sailors who steered north found Iceland and Greenland so green and gracious that they could scarcely believe their eyes.\n\nThe Martian astronomers, for there are astronomers on Mars though they are very different beings from us, followed the visitor with their instruments all the way home. One of them wrote afterward, \"Considering the mass and temperature of the missile that was flung into our sun, it is astonishing what a little damage the earth, which it missed so narrowly, has managed to sustain.\"\n\nWhich only shows how small the vastest of human troubles may look, from a distance of a few million miles.",
