        'synopsis' => 'On an Alaskan glacier in a rising storm, the explorer John Muir and a tiny stray dog must cross a knife of ice above a crevasse, and only one of them is afraid.',
        'text' => "In the summer of 1880 I set out from Fort Wrangel in a canoe to explore the icy region of southeastern Alaska. My Indian crew was ready, provisions were stowed, friends were waving from the wharf, and there at the last moment came Mr. Taylor's little black dog, begging so hard for a place that I could not refuse him.\n\nStickeen, as he was called, was the least promising traveler imaginable. He was small enough to hide under a camp stool, with short legs and a curled tail, and he said almost nothing on the whole voyage up the coast. The other dogs bullied him. The rain soaked him. Yet every morning when we pushed ashore he trotted after me over the boulders with his ears up, silent and curious, keeping himself to himself like a fox.\n\nOn the third day I left camp alone for a long ramble across the glacier, meaning to be back by evening. Stickeen followed without being asked, as usual. We had gone far among the blue crevasses and ice-cliffs when the storm found us. Rain fell in floods, then turned to snow driven sideways by a wind that made the very rocks lean away from it. Daylight began to fail while we were still miles from the woods.\n\nI hurried back toward camp, crossing gashes in the ice that would have stopped me in calm weather, and Stickeen jumped them all like a creature made for the work. But at last our way was barred by a crevasse too wide for any jump, and after tracing it upstream and down I found we were trapped on an island of ice with one possible way out, a slender bridge spanning the great chasm.\n\nIt was a sliver bridge such as glaciers sometimes leave where two cracks overlap. Seventy feet long it curved across a gulf fifty feet wide, worn by ages of melting to the thinness of a knife blade, sagging down in the middle and rising again toward either side. Below us lay the blue darkness of the valley of the shadow of death. Above us swept the storm. Night was close behind it.\n\nI cut steps down the icy wall with my axe and crossed inch by inch, astride the blade, chipping footholds, ignoring the abyss on either hand. When I reached the other side I called to Stickeen. And now the little coward became the strangest thing I ever met in all my wanderings.\n\nHe looked down into that gulf, looked at the trembling sliver, and looked into my face, and cried. There is no other word for it. He ran in circles seeking another way, came back, moaned at the brink, and begged me with his whole shaking body not to go into that awful place. \"Hush your fears, my boy,\" I told him. \"We will get across safe, though it is not going to be easy. No right way is easy in this rough world. We must risk our lives to save them.\"\n\nBut nothing I could say would bring him to the edge. At last I hardened my heart and shouted across that I must leave him, that if he would not come I could only return tomorrow to seek him, and that the wolves of the woods would make short work of a lost dog tonight.\n\nThat decided him. With the courage of despair he crept to the brink, pressed his body flat, slid his four little feet together down into my first cut step, then slowly, counting each hold like a pendulum, worked his way along the blade of the bridge through the wind. At the foot of the wall below me he halted, studied the notches I had chipped as if fixing each one in his mind, and then in one springy rush hooked his paws from step to step and whizzed past my head, safe!\n\nAnd now came a scene! Well done, brave boy, I cried, trying to catch him. But he would not be caught. Never have I seen such a revulsion from despair to joy. He flashed and darted about the ice screaming and shouting, swirling in giddy loops like a leaf in a whirlwind, rolling over and over, sobbing and laughing in his strange dog voice, crying plainly as speech, \"Saved! Saved! Saved!\" I feared he would die of happiness, and indeed the passion of it was enough to kill anything smaller than his gladness.\n\nThere is nothing like work for toning down excess, so I ran ahead gruffly calling him on, for night was falling and miles of broken ice still lay between us and the trees. We reached camp about ten o'clock, too tired even to eat much. That night both the dog and I sprang up from sleep, muttering, dreaming of the brink.\n\nAfterward Stickeen was a changed dog. He would hardly take food from any hand but mine. In the evenings by the fire he would rest his head on my knee and look up as if asking, \"Wasn't that an awful time we had together on the glacier?\"\n\nHis master wrote me later that in the summer of 1883 a tourist stole him away at Fort Wrangel on some steamer, and what became of him no one knows. Doubtless he has crossed the last crevasse and gone on before. But he will not be forgotten. To me Stickeen is immortal.",
